<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For contextualization, we leverage state-space models instead of self-attention. Throughout the paper, <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sequence length, <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> the embedding dimension and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> the dimension of the state-space hidden state (to be introduced in <a href="#S3" title="3 Background ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Before delving into our model in <a href="#S4" title="4 Model ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we describe below
the main components of the state-space architecture and elaborate on their potential for long sequence processing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">State-space models.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For unidimensional inputs <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}=(u_{0},...,u_{L-1})\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}=(u_{0},...,u_{L-1})\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, deep SSMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2022b</a>)</cite> are based on the recurrent equation:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{aligned} {\bm{x}}_{j+1}&amp;={\bm{A}}{\bm{x}}_{j}+{\bm{b}}u_{j+1},\\
y_{j+1}&amp;={\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{x}}_{j+1}+du_{j+1},\end{aligned}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{aligned} {\bm{x}}_{j+1}&amp;={\bm{A}}{\bm{x}}_{j}+{\bm{b}}u_{j+1},\\
y_{j+1}&amp;={\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{x}}_{j+1}+du_{j+1},\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the SSM hidden state and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> the output of the SSM. The state matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> carries and transforms the hidden state through the iterations along with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which are learned parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">State-space convolution.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By unrolling the recurrence above, the output sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as: <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}=\sum_{l=0}^{j}{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{A}}^{j-l}{\bm{b}}u_{l}+du_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}=\sum_{l=0}^{j}{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{A}}^{j-l}{\bm{b}}u_{l}+du_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall l\in\{1,...,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall l\in\{1,...,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> denote the causal convolution operator (details about this operator are in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Convolution ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). Then, we can define a convolution kernel <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\kappa}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\kappa}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> that depends on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ssm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>SSM</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ssm</annotation></semantics></math> layer is therefore parametrized by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> and its output is defined by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as in the following equation:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{aligned} {\bm{y}}&amp;=\bm{\kappa}*{\bm{u}}+d{\bm{u}},\\
\bm{\kappa}&amp;=\left({\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{A}}{\bm{b}},\dots,{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{A}}^{L-1}{\bm{b}}\right).\end{aligned}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>𝒚</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜿</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>𝜿</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{aligned} {\bm{y}}&amp;=\bm{\kappa}*{\bm{u}}+d{\bm{u}},\\
\bm{\kappa}&amp;=\left({\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{A}}{\bm{b}},\dots,{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{A}}^{L-1}{\bm{b}}\right).\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For multidimensional <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H}</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply compute <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> convolutions with one kernel <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\kappa}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜿</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\kappa}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> for each dimension.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SSMs efficiency.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the linear time-dependency between hidden states, as shown in <a href="#S3.E1" title="In State-space models. ‣ 3 Background ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we can compute the whole output <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> directly as a convolution, without iteration over the time dimension, as opposed to RNNs.
A naive implementation of (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ State-space convolution. ‣ 3 Background ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) would incur a quadratic complexity in the input length <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the complexity of transformers and thus be prohibitive for long sequences. However, thanks to the FFT, this computation can be performed in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(L\log L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(L\log L)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Convolution ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for more details).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Model</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the LOCOST model.
We first introduce the bidirectional deep state-space model, then show how to use it to enable global contextualization of the tokens. Then, we present the architecture of the LOCOST layer with an efficient contextualization that can be used as a drop-in replacement for the self-attention mechanism in transformers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.17919v3/archi-longssmv3.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/569;" width="476" height="569"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>The LOCOST layer.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.17919v3/gated-ffv1.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/546;" width="476" height="546"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Gated feedforward net.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The embedded sequence is contextualized via a gated bidirectional SSM before passing through a gated feedforward net.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Capturing local and global contexts</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intuition.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In deep SSMs, information from previous tokens flows up to the current token through the hidden states <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The convolution view provides another angle: each output <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted sum of the previous tokens <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}_{0},\dots,{\bm{u}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}_{0},\dots,{\bm{u}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose weights are given by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bidirectional contextualization.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To aggregate information from both directions, we consider bidirectional convolutions. A first kernel, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}</annotation></semantics></math> performs the regular causal convolution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}*{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}*{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A second kernel <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overrightarrow{\bm{\kappa}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overrightarrow{\bm{\kappa}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to compute the cross-correlation with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The results of these two operations are summed out (similar to bi-recurrent encoder). The overall operation is described by the following equation:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{y}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{y}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{l\leq j}\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}_{j-l}\odot{\bm{u}}_{l}+\sum_{l\geq j}\overrightarrow{\bm{\kappa}}_{l-j}\odot{\bm{u}}_{l}+{\bm{d}}\odot{\bm{u}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{l\leq j}\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}_{j-l}\odot{\bm{u}}_{l}+\sum_{l\geq j}\overrightarrow{\bm{\kappa}}_{l-j}\odot{\bm{u}}_{l}+{\bm{d}}\odot{\bm{u}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bissm({\bm{U}})_{j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">BiSSM</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bissm({\bm{U}})_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In this equation, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{U}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{U}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding matrix of the input text: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{u}}_{0},\dots,{\bm{u}}_{L-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{u}}_{0},\dots,{\bm{u}}_{L-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The kernels <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overrightarrow{\bm{\kappa}},\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜿</mi><mo>←</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overrightarrow{\bm{\kappa}},\overleftarrow{\bm{\kappa}}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed as in <a href="#S3.E2" title="In State-space convolution. ‣ 3 Background ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, with their respective parameters <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overrightarrow{{\bm{A}}},\overrightarrow{{\bm{c}}},\overrightarrow{{\bm{b}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overrightarrow{{\bm{A}}},\overrightarrow{{\bm{c}}},\overrightarrow{{\bm{b}}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overleftarrow{{\bm{A}}},\overleftarrow{{\bm{c}}},\overleftarrow{{\bm{b}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overleftarrow{{\bm{A}}},\overleftarrow{{\bm{c}}},\overleftarrow{{\bm{b}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The element-wise product is denoted by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> and we consider multidimensional inputs, with one kernel per dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The output <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is now contextualized as a weighted sum of previous <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}_{\leq j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}_{\leq j}</annotation></semantics></math> and subsequent <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}_{\geq j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}_{\geq j}</annotation></semantics></math> inputs. For scalar inputs, more insights on how far in the future or in the past a scalar input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to the scalar output <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by the spectral radii
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\overrightarrow{{\bm{A}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\overrightarrow{{\bm{A}}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\overleftarrow{{\bm{A}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\overleftarrow{{\bm{A}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed the sensitivity of an output <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to an input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by the following quantity:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left|\dfrac{\partial y_{j}}{\partial u_{l}}\right|\leq\begin{cases}\rho(\overleftarrow{{\bm{A}}})^{j-l}\lvert\overleftarrow{{\bm{c}}}^{\top}\overleftarrow{{\bm{b}}}\rvert&amp;\text{if}\quad l&lt;j,\\
\rho(\overrightarrow{{\bm{A}}})^{l-j}\lvert\overrightarrow{{\bm{c}}}^{\top}\overrightarrow{{\bm{b}}}\rvert&amp;\text{if}\quad l&gt;j.\end{cases}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>←</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>→</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left|\dfrac{\partial y_{j}}{\partial u_{l}}\right|\leq\begin{cases}\rho(\overleftarrow{{\bm{A}}})^{j-l}\lvert\overleftarrow{{\bm{c}}}^{\top}\overleftarrow{{\bm{b}}}\rvert&amp;\text{if}\quad l&lt;j,\\
\rho(\overrightarrow{{\bm{A}}})^{l-j}\lvert\overrightarrow{{\bm{c}}}^{\top}\overrightarrow{{\bm{b}}}\rvert&amp;\text{if}\quad l&gt;j.\end{cases}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.17919v3/kernels_zoom.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/341;" width="452" height="341"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Visualization of the kernels corresponding to the first dimension for several layers of the pre-trained model. Bins show the average decay of the forward and backward kernels. This illustrates the different scales of each kernel. Layers 1 and 10 capture short and extra-short range contextualizations, while Layers 4 and 7 model extra-long and long contexts, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For multidimensional inputs, using a state-space kernel for each dimension enables a fine-grained adjustment of the spectral radii independently for each of them. A small value corresponds to modeling local contexts, while a large value captures global ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Some of the corresponding kernel weights of this convolution can be visualized on <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Bidirectional contextualization. ‣ 4.1 Capturing local and global contexts ‣ 4 Model ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. A more complete visualization can be found in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Visualisation of learned kernels ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Architecture</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Encoder.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our encoder consists of a stack of LOCOST layers, illustrated in <a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4 Model ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>. It is computed as follows:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Embedding matrix <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{U}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{U}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H}</annotation></semantics></math> is first projected onto <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}},{\bm{V}}\in\mathbb{C}^{L\times H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}},{\bm{V}}\in\mathbb{C}^{L\times H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is contextualized through a <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bissm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>BiSSM</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bissm</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A pointwise multiplication <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}\odot\bissm({\bm{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0em">BiSSM</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}\odot\bissm({\bm{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a first gate before passing the output through a feedforward layer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This feedforward layer employs a second gating mechanism (see <a href="#S4.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4 Model ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>). For this component, we use gated GeLU that has shown to be efficient by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The architecture of the LOCOST layer (<a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4 Model ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>) resembles that of a transformer layer except that the self-attention mechanism is replaced by a gated bidirectional state-space model. We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. (2022a)</a></cite> for the parametrization and initialization of the state-space models (more details in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E State-space models implementation details ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decoder.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since our focus is on long input summarization, the generation output length is very short compared to the input. For decoding, we follow the practice of other efficient architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and use a vanilla transformer decoder equipped with dense self- and cross-attention. A full description of hyperparameters of the model is provided in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Hyperparameters ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Complexity.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LOCOST layer takes <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(H^{2}L+HNL+HL\log L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(H^{2}L+HNL+HL\log L)</annotation></semantics></math> time and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(HNL)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(HNL)</annotation></semantics></math> space to compute. We refer to <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Computational complexity of a LOCOST layer ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our experiments, we focus on the long document abstractive summarization task as it represents a typical conditional generation problem with long input requirements.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approach.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate LOCOST following a classical pre-training then fine-tuning approach. For fine-tuning, we used the official train, validation and test splits of each dataset. We train all models until convergence and select the best model based on the validation Mean ROUGE (mean of ROUGE-1/2/LSum) for test evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate LOCOST both with reference-based and reference-free metrics. For reference-based summarization evaluation, we use the traditional n-gram overlap summarization metrics <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ROUGE-1/2/Lsum</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, 2004</a>)</cite>. We average them into a single score to compare with other baselines. We also report <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BERTScore</span> (BS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang* et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, a model-based metric. For reference-free evaluation, we report the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BLANC</span> (BL) score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vasilyev et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, a metric that has been shown to correlate well with human evaluations. We also assess the throughput (samples per second) and the memory usage (MiB of GPU RAM) of LOCOST compared with other state-of-the-art sparse transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In all of our experiments, we intentionally favored simplicity and opted for greedy decoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Pre-training</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pre-training objective.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To pre-train the model, we leverage the gap-sentences generation (GSG) unsupervised pre-training objective, which was introduced by PEGASUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and is well-suited for sequence-to-sequence generation. Unlike BART <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite> or T5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>)</cite> pre-training objectives, GSG endows the model with zero-shot summarization capabilities. GSG was successfully applied by subsequent generation models such as LongT5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and PEGASUS-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Phang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Namely, a document <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is split into its <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> sentences: <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a ratio <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, GSG then identifies <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\lfloor\alpha M\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\lfloor\alpha M\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> sentences from <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that maximize the ROUGE-1 (noted <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-}1</annotation></semantics></math>) with the rest of the document:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\argtopk_{j}\&gt;R\text{-}1\bigl(\bigcup_{i\neq j}\{s_{i}\},s_{j}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>top</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\argtopk_{j}\&gt;R\text{-}1\bigl(\bigcup_{i\neq j}\{s_{i}\},s_{j}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The resulting subset <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\{1,\ldots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\{1,\ldots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math> splits the document into a pseudo summary <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}=\{s_{i}\}_{i\in U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}=\{s_{i}\}_{i\in U}</annotation></semantics></math> and a pseudo-source <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}=\{s_{i}\}_{i\notin U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}=\{s_{i}\}_{i\notin U}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are used for pre-training with the standard cross-entropy loss.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pre-training data.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We pre-train the model exclusively on the C4 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, in BF16 for 1M steps, using an input sequence length of 4,096 and an output sequence length of 910.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pre-training optimization.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The learning rate scheduler we use is identical to T5, employing an inverse square root function, with the warm-up steps set to 10,000. We set the GSG-ratio <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and do not employ dropout during this phase. We follow closely the same pre-training as LongT5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
