<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative evaluation: GPT-3.5 preference.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since our input texts are very long, performing a full human-based evaluation would be very costly and time consuming. Instead, we perform a mock human evaluation using GPT-3.5 <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">*</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              We use <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gpt-3.5-turbo-16k</span> model for evaluation.</span></span></span>. This practice has been used and has shown success in summary evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilardi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang and Lee, 2023</a>)</cite>. We ask the model to rate the generated summary on four dimensions: <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relevance</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consistency</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fluency</span>, and <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coherence</span>. More details are given in <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I GPT-3.5 evaluation ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We perform evaluation on 500 samples randomly taken from PubMed. The results are shown in <a href="#S5.T5" title="In Qualitative evaluation: GPT-3.5 preference. ‣ 5.4 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LOCOST: State-Space Models for Long Document Abstractive Summarization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. LOCOST produces summaries at a competitive level with respect to LongT5 (93-97%).</p>
</div>
